1244:| OD129 | <a id="od129"></a>Type arguments of an enum literal taken from its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an enum literal or variant written without type arguments (`Option::Some { value: 42 }`, `Option::None`) takes them from the type its position expects, and from which positions. Bears on TYPE-7, TYPE-15 and OPEN-46, which asks the same of a generic call's type arguments and whose pick excludes the expected result type. The current compiler: the type arguments come from the expected type of a `return` (the function's result type), of an annotated `let` and of a call argument (the parameter's type); all three exit 42 on both consumers, and spec examples rely on it (PAT-1). The syntax freeze review (§1.3, kill-list items 1, 4 and 5) proposed this inference for enum literals and for constructor calls such as `Vec::new()`. Options: (a) explicit type arguments always; (b) from the expected type in the positions the current compiler uses; (c) the answer to OPEN-46, applied to enum literals. Austral: type parameters are inferred from the arguments, and a declaration's type can disambiguate. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-129 | see OPEN-129 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Same rule as OD46: type arguments from the arguments, otherwise explicit (`None::<i32>`) |
