1267:| OD152 | <a id="od152"></a>Whether a path's first letter decides its syntactic form (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether the first letter of a path's first segment decides the syntactic form of `X::y`, or one path form leaves to name resolution whether `X` is a type or a module. Bears on SYN-10, SYN-1 and MOD-1. The current compiler: the parser chooses by the first letter of `X`: an uppercase `X` gives an associated-function call or an enum literal, and a lowercase `X` the flattened name `X_y`, which name resolution looks up as one identifier (bug 343); a module named with an uppercase letter goes through the associated-function form, and `M::f()` on `mod M` builds and runs on both consumers. SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 presents the case split as how the parse stays LL(1). Options: (a) the first letter decides the syntactic form, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 states it; (b) the parser reads `X::y` as one path form, which is also LL(1), and name resolution decides whether `X` is a type or a module. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-152 | see OPEN-152 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Resolve `X::y` by declaration, not by the first letter |
