153:<a id="FFI-4"></a>
154:### FFI-4. Layout attributes are well formed
156:**FFI-4@1.** A `#[repr(C)]` struct is not generic and has only FFI-safe fields, `#[repr(packed)]` and `#[repr(align(N))]` are not combined, and `N` is a power of two no larger than the struct's natural alignment.
269:<a id="FFI-7"></a>
270:### FFI-7. Layout is unspecified unless a `repr` fixes it
272:**FFI-7@1.** The field order, offsets, padding, size and alignment of a struct or enum are unspecified except as a struct's `#[repr]` attribute fixes them (FFI-8), and safe code observes them only through `sizeof::<T>()` and `alignof::<T>()`, which give `T`'s size and alignment in bytes as a `u64`.
304:<a id="FFI-8"></a>
305:### FFI-8. What `repr(C)` and `repr(packed)` promise
307:**FFI-8@2.** `#[repr(C)]` lays a struct out as C does, each field in declaration order at the next offset aligned for its type and the size rounded up to the struct's alignment, and `#[repr(packed)]` places the fields in declaration order with no padding and gives the struct alignment 1, accepts misaligned fields but refuses references to them (also when combined with `repr(C)`).
320:it changes nothing (FFI-4, E0585). Which combinations are well formed is FFI-4. OD111 accepts packed fields at misaligned offsets and refuses references to them; the current compiler still accepts such references.
397:**FFI-10@1.** The only layout attributes are `#[repr(C)]`, `#[repr(packed)]` and `#[repr(align(N))]`, alone or combined as FFI-4 allows and written only on a struct, so every other struct has the unspecified layout of FFI-7, and no other `repr` option, `transparent` included, and no `repr` on an enum is accepted.
518: This is the one layout fact beyond FFI-7 and FFI-8 that trusted code needs to walk a
