170:<a id="decided-2026-11-05"></a>
171:## Owner decisions 2026-11-05: Core design (A2a provisional approval) and project rows
177:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-revisions"></a>**Owner-approved revisions of recorded rules** (each also noted at the rule it revises):
185:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
186:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q9-1-reads"></a>**Q9.1, read forms** (owner-confirmed, 2026-11-05): `copy x` is allowed only when `x` has a Copy type and `move x` only when it has a non-Copy (linear) type. Each read has one form; `move` of a Copy value and `copy` of a linear value are both refused.
188:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-new-rules"></a>**New rules** (P4): duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl are refused, like duplicate fields (bug 313); the rule gets its ID and diagnostic when it is written in Lean.
190:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-core"></a>**Core rows** (the answer is the row's recommendation):
206:- **Q2.2** Which containers implement `Destroy`? — Exactly sixteen named Destroy impls: Array (T: Destroy); Heap, HeapArray and Vec (two each: T: Copy; T: Destroy); String (unconditional); Option (T: Destroy); Result (three: T: Destroy/E: Copy, T: Destroy/E: Destroy, T: Copy/E: Destroy); OrderedMap (four: each of K and V Copy or Destroy). HashMap excluded. <a id="q2-2-revision"></a>Revised 2026-11-06 by [information flow](#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06): `Secret<T>` is `Copy` only for scalar `T`; secret arrays and heap secrets are linear and their destruction wipes them.
255:- **Q8.2** What exact source subset is admitted? — A1 minimal `u8`/linear-struct component subset. <a id="q8-2-revision"></a>Confirmed 2026-11-06 by [vertical slices](#decided-project-vertical-slices-2026-11-06): this is slice 1; its defined trap is an explicit `abort()`, with the refused `200 + 56` as its known-violation control; integers are complete at slice 2.
273:- **Q9.13** Are judgments independent of the checker and elaborator? — Independent judgments; checker/elaborator correspondence.
281:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-project"></a>**Project rows:**
288:- **P6** Retention manifest gates deleting legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv at the root gives every legacy asset (corpus, regressions, examples, std, bug ledger, site, docs, research) a destination and deletion criterion. Revised 2026-11-06 ([restart row](#decided-project-restart)): nothing is deleted at B3; legacy/, the old pipeline included, is deleted way later (LD: once the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB, ethrex or similar), when RETENTION.tsv has no live entry, a final ideas sweep has dispositioned every idea and the owner explicitly approves.
